```

It zipped the **entire repository** (including `.git` and any secrets in history) and uploaded it to an external server (`hackerX.com`) on every push to `main`. It also used outdated actions (`checkout@v3`, `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3`) and a nonexistent `npm run build` step.

I replaced it with a minimal, least-privilege workflow that just uploads the static site to GitHub Pages:

- `actions/checkout@v4` → `actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3` → `actions/deploy-pages@v4`
- Explicit `permissions:` block (`contents: read`, `pages: write`, `id-token: write`)
- `environment: github-pages` (so you can add required reviewers in repo settings)
- No external URLs, no archiving, no `npm`

**To activate:** in the repo, go to **Settings → Pages → Source: "Deploy from a branch" → select "GitHub Actions"**.

## Push commands (dummy URL)

Since the repo isn't a git repository locally yet, initialize it first. Replace `https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPO.git` with your real URL:

```bash
cd my_website

# If this folder is not yet a git repo:
git init -b main
git add .
git commit -m "Add personal research website (About, Blog, Publications) and safe Pages workflow"

# If it already has a remote, just:
# git add . && git commit -m "..."

git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPO.git
git push -u origin main
```

Best practices applied:
- **Authenticate with a Personal Access Token or `gh auth login`**, never a password — put the token in the URL (`https://<TOKEN>@github.com/...`) or, better, use a credential helper so it isn't stored in `.git/config`.
- **Don't commit secrets.** If the malicious workflow ever ran with secrets in the repo, rotate any exposed tokens.
- **Review the workflow before pushing** — done above; the new one only uses official `actions/*` and writes only to the Pages branch.
- After pushing, check **Actions** to confirm the deploy runs green, then visit `https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/YOUR-REPO/`.

